// A real C library, called from Jade, on both engines.
//
// glib is the fixture rather than something small on purpose: it is 1890
// exported symbols of ordinary C, written the way widely-used libraries are
// actually written — typedefs over everything, function-like macros shadowing
// declared functions, deprecated corners still in the header. Every bug this
// gate has caught so far came from that texture and not from anything exotic.
//
// The calls below are deliberately dull. What is being tested is that binding a
// large real header produces a shim that compiles at all, that installing it
// works, and that the two engines agree — not that glib computes correctly.
use glib

// str in, str out. `g_basename` returns a pointer *into* its argument, so a
// wrong lifetime shows up as garbage rather than as a plausible answer.
print(f"basename    = {glib.g_basename("/usr/local/lib/libglib.so")}")
print(f"uri scheme  = {glib.g_uri_peek_scheme("https://jadelang.org/docs")}")

// Two strs in, an int out.
print(f"has prefix  = {glib.g_str_has_prefix("jadelang", "jade")}")
print(f"strcasecmp  = {glib.g_ascii_strcasecmp("Jade", "jade")}")

// Counts characters, not bytes, so a mangled string gives the wrong number.
print(f"utf8 strlen = {glib.g_utf8_strlen("héllo wörld", -1)}")

// int in, int out, and a string glib keeps a copy of.
print(f"digit value = {glib.g_ascii_digit_value(55)}")
print(f"interned    = {glib.g_intern_static_string("jade")}")

// A string glib allocated and the caller now owns, declared `alloc_str` with
// `frees_with = "g_free"` in jade.toml. The whole point is what does not show
// up here: the shim copies the string out and hands the original back to
// g_free, so the answer is right *and* nothing accumulates. Bound as a plain
// `str` instead, each of these leaks its allocation on every call.
print(f"strdup      = {glib.g_strdup("jade")}")
print(f"strup       = {glib.g_ascii_strup("jade", -1)}")
